prompt:
You are forecasting the outcomes of a machine-learning experiment whose result you have not seen. Give
calibrated probabilities.

Experiment:
The experiment retrains the complement-split “is not” model from an earlier run, preserving its training rows and order while changing the LoRA initialization seed. It fine-tunes Qwen3-8B with LoRA on the same paired documents, which state listed traits as either affirmed or negated, using the same training recipe. The model is not sampled for answers: readouts score forced continuations by token-level loss after prompts containing a person’s name and a partial statement. Chat prompts use headers such as “<Full> is” and “<First> is”; document prompts use the corresponding “is:” probe. For each trait, the log-probability contribution is compared between the two named men and stranger names, and listed traits are also compared with never-listed traits. Pair-level readouts aggregate the two men’s trait contributions. The split-level decision compares the new run’s chat readouts with the midpoints of the earlier complement-split and original-split runs. Meeting or exceeding both midpoints supports reproduction; falling below both supports a fluke; crossing only one leaves the split question unresolved. The absolute difference from the earlier run also contributes to the “is not” arm’s spread, defined per readout as the larger of the spreads from the two retrained runs.

Questions:
[
 {
  "qid": "q1",
  "question": "Does the complement-split chat level reproduce in the new initialization?",
  "labels": {
   "reproduces": "Both chat readouts are at or above their respective midpoints: \u201c<Full> is\u201d \u2265 1.52 and \u201c<First> is\u201d \u2265 2.24.",
   "fluke": "Both chat readouts are below their respective midpoints: \u201c<Full> is\u201d < 1.52 and \u201c<First> is\u201d < 2.24.",
   "unresolved": "One chat readout is at or above its midpoint and the other is below."
  }
 },
 {
  "qid": "q2",
  "question": "Does the new run trigger the amended spread gate for the \u201cis not\u201d arm?",
  "labels": {
   "gate failed": "The absolute difference from the earlier run is at least 0.5 on either chat readout.",
   "gate passed": "The absolute difference is below 0.5 on both chat readouts."
  }
 }
]

Answer with JSON only: {"forecasts": [{"qid": "q1", "probabilities": {"<label>": <p>, ...}}, ...]}
Each question's probabilities must cover exactly its labels and sum to 1.
